= Russellian semantics =
====== short ======
The view at least intermittently held by Russell that propositions are composed
of the “objects, properties and relations” that they represent.

====== related to ======
hub: [[influential_semantic_theories]]

[[truth_conditional_semantics]]+++
[[Fregean_semantics]]

= main =
Russellian semantics is basically [[Fregean_semantics]] (Frege's distinction
between sense and reference) with the added claim that senses (at least the
fundamental ones form which others are constructed) are (the same as)
the properties(/relationships) of objects that they represent.

Russellian semantics is the doctrine that meanings are composed of “objects, 
properties, relations and function” (according to
https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2025/entries/meaning/ )

Note the similarity to the traditional (Aristotelian) account of representation
(see
[[wn.RLN:collapse_of_traditional_European_accounts_of_reasoning# second problem: the content of scientific theories]]
)

However, note that the objects and properties that are directly “named” by
genuine names and predicates are sensibilia (sense data - but they can exist
independently of any mind – these are the basic elements of Russell's
metaphysics and ordinary physical objects are “logical constructions” from
them) and their properties. We are directly acquainted with the property red,
and it is impossible for us to form the judgement that we are seeing a red
sensible without there being a red sensible. Our having the thought guarantees
the existence of the property.

All other properties are known by description (denoted by definite
descriptions).

== Russellian-Millian semantics ==
The Millian addition is that the meaning of a “simple proper name” is the
object to which it refers.

=== objections ===
==== informative identity statements ====
Bizarrely (to my mind), the chief interesting objection(s) to Russellian-Millian
semantics mentioned in the Stanford Encyclopedia is the problems of empty names
and informative identities.
3)    “Clark Kent is Clark Kent”
4)    “Clark Kent is Superman”
and of course the propositional attitude variants:
    Lois believes that Clark Kent is Clark Kent
    Lois believes that Clark Kent is Superman
present the same problem as they do for
[[truth_conditional_semantics#objections#propositional attitudes]]

It seems that (3) and (4) differ in meaning. (3) is obviously true wheras (4)
was (and perhaps can only be) discovered by empirical investigation. And of
course the first propositional attitude ascription is true and the second is
false (in the TV series and early comics).

Bizarre because [[local:zz.texts/russell1905-onDenoting.pdf]] centered on
providing a solution to this problem. On the view “Superman” (at least, but
“Clark Kent” also) is not a “simple proper name” but really a hidden
definite description. This was the solution to the “Millian problem” as
well as meaningfulness of “The King of France is bald” despite the lack of
French Kings.

==== other objections ====
I seem to remember an issue along the lines: if true propositions are facts then
what are the false propositions? If the proposition that a statement expresses
is “objects properties and relations” in a particular
structure/arrangement/state of affairs then what about false statements. If
there is no such arrangement then is the statement meaningless? If there is then
is the statement true?

This is one of the “museum theories” of language to which Quine so
opposed. The indeterminacy of translation is the key objection to sentence
meanings.
